            <item>
                <title>Hackers got a four-day head start on epic PHP2Shell vulnerability</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/hackers-got-a-four-day-head-start-on-epic-php2shell-vulnerability/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/hackers-got-a-four-day-head-start-on-epic-php2shell-vulnerability/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Tom Wilson and Rafe Rosner-Uddin, Financial Times]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 15:25:02 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[php]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[PHP exploit]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/hackers-got-a-four-day-head-start-on-epic-php2shell-vulnerability/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Hackers were already probing the bug before defenders were told it existed.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Amazon has warned that <a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/php-maintainers-tell-web-devs-to-prepare-themselves-for-critical-security-patches/">vulnerabilities in a programming language</a> that underpins much of the Internet, discovered using AI tools, are being “broadly exploited” as governments and companies rush to secure their systems.</p>
<p>The world’s largest cloud computing provider on Wednesday said it had identified an attempt by hackers to exploit the bug four days before the weakness was disclosed to the public.</p>
<p>Amazon added the flaws were “now being broadly exploited in the wild.”</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/hackers-got-a-four-day-head-start-on-epic-php2shell-vulnerability/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/hackers-got-a-four-day-head-start-on-epic-php2shell-vulnerability/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>46</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/internet-routing-1000x648.jpeg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1000" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/internet-routing-500x500.jpeg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>The new human-crafted Apple Music brand matters in an AI world</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/culture/2026/10/the-new-human-crafted-apple-music-brand-matters-in-an-ai-world/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/culture/2026/10/the-new-human-crafted-apple-music-brand-matters-in-an-ai-world/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Aurich Lawson]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 19:44:40 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Apple]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Culture]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[apple]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[opinion]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/culture/2026/10/the-new-human-crafted-apple-music-brand-matters-in-an-ai-world/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Opinion: In a world of AI slop, showing the human hands making things helps.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Apple just revealed a new brand identity for Apple Music, with the expected turn to Liquid Glass. I’m not much of a fan of their new design language in general; this is where I admit that I’m still running MacOS Sequoia and have grumpy things to say about rounded corner radii and text legibility.</p>
<p>It’s just a trend, it will pass, and at some point I’ll probably cave and upgrade when it starts actually affecting my ability to get work done, or more importantly get security updates. Once I adjust, I’ll stop noticing it, and life will go on.</p>
<p>As such, I wouldn’t normally find an obvious rebrand like this notable, much less feel like I had much to say about it. But then I happened to catch Apple’s behind-the-scenes video in my YouTube recommendations, and I realized I did have thoughts.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/culture/2026/10/the-new-human-crafted-apple-music-brand-matters-in-an-ai-world/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/culture/2026/10/the-new-human-crafted-apple-music-brand-matters-in-an-ai-world/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>102</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/apple-music-rebrand-icon-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/apple-music-rebrand-icon-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Apple</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>OpenAI&#039;s latest AI models are coming to Google Cloud and Antigravity</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openais-latest-ai-models-are-coming-to-google-cloud-and-antigravity/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openais-latest-ai-models-are-coming-to-google-cloud-and-antigravity/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ryan Whitwam]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 18:51:42 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[google cloud]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[openai]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openais-latest-ai-models-are-coming-to-google-cloud-and-antigravity/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Google Cloud will have GPT 6.1 Astra, Sol, and Luna in the coming weeks. ]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Google would certainly prefer that everyone used its Gemini models with Google Cloud services, but soon, you'll have more options. OpenAI and Google have <a href="https://openai.com/index/openai-models-google-cloud/">come to an arrangement</a> that will see the former's frontier models arrive in Google Cloud services. Perhaps more importantly, GPT models will soon appear in Antigravity, Google's agent-powered coding tool.</p>
<p>For enterprise customers and AI enthusiasts, OpenAI's latest model will appear in Google's "Model Garden" in the coming weeks, adding to its 200-plus options. The garden already offers access to Google's Gemini models, as well as an assortment of open models like Mistral, Llama, and Gemma. Anthropic's Claude AI has also been available for some time. <a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/google-cloud-brings-openai-models-to-customers">Google says</a> it's now the only large cloud provider offering access to all three leading closed LLMs.</p>
<p>While OpenAI notes that Microsoft continues to be its primary cloud partner, the full suite of current GPT 6 and 6.1 models—Astra, Sol, and Luna—will launch on Google Cloud. Astra will naturally cost the most to run, but Google's system supports dynamically moving workloads between LLMs based on performance, latency, and efficiency.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openais-latest-ai-models-are-coming-to-google-cloud-and-antigravity/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openais-latest-ai-models-are-coming-to-google-cloud-and-antigravity/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>12</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Google-cloud-IO-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Google-cloud-IO-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Ryan Whitwam</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Judge rejects Trump’s wild claim EPA can dismiss data center pollution lawsuits</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/judge-rejects-trumps-wild-claim-epa-can-dismiss-data-center-pollution-lawsuits/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/judge-rejects-trumps-wild-claim-epa-can-dismiss-data-center-pollution-lawsuits/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 18:20:40 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[clean air act]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[colossus data center]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[data center protests]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Donald Trump]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Elon Musk]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Environmental Protection Agency]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[EPA]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[pollution]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[SpaceXAI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[xAI]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/judge-rejects-trumps-wild-claim-epa-can-dismiss-data-center-pollution-lawsuits/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Trump’s EPA can't unilaterally dismiss SpaceXAI pollution lawsuit, court rules.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Elon Musk’s SpaceXAI is stuck facing a lawsuit over alleged data center pollution after Donald Trump failed to convince a judge that his Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) has complete control over all Clean Air Act lawsuits and can dismiss any suit at whim.</p>
<p>Soon after <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2025/04/elon-musks-xai-accused-of-lying-to-black-communities-about-harmful-pollution/">unpermitted gas turbines were discovered</a> powering Musk’s giant Colossus data center in Memphis, Tennessee, the <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2025/06/xai-faces-legal-threat-over-alleged-colossus-data-center-pollution-in-memphis/">NAACP sued</a>. Its lawsuit sought an injunction requiring xAI (which, following a <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/02/spacex-acquires-xai-plans-1-million-satellite-constellation-to-power-it/">merger</a>, is known now as SpaceXAI) to use the best available pollution control technology when operating the turbines and ordering penalties up to $124,426 per day per violation. Without such remedies, the turbines risked drastically spiking pollution in already vulnerable neighborhoods housing predominantly Black communities nearby, the NAACP argued.</p>
<p>To block the lawsuit, Trump tried to intervene, arguing that the EPA had the power to unilaterally dismiss lawsuits any time the president decides against enforcing the Clean Air Act. According to Trump, it would supposedly “not serve the public interest” if citizens were allowed to seek massive civil penalties to punish polluting AI firms that were trying to get data centers operational as fast as possible to support <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2025/07/white-house-unveils-sweeping-plan-to-win-global-ai-race-through-deregulation/">Trump’s AI Action Plan</a>.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/judge-rejects-trumps-wild-claim-epa-can-dismiss-data-center-pollution-lawsuits/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/judge-rejects-trumps-wild-claim-epa-can-dismiss-data-center-pollution-lawsuits/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>40</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/xAI-Thermal-Image-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/xAI-Thermal-Image-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>©Steve Jones, Flight by Southwings for SELC</media:credit><media:text>Thermal imaging of xAI's Memphis facility prompted allegations that more than 30 of xAI’s methane gas turbines are operating without environmental permitting.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>The $15 billion ad-powered AI startup your doctor may already be using</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/the-15-billion-ad-powered-ai-startup-your-doctor-may-already-be-using/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/the-15-billion-ad-powered-ai-startup-your-doctor-may-already-be-using/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Rafe Rosner-Uddin, Financial Times]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 15:14:07 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Health]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI and health care]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI health]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[health care]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[healthcare]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[OpenEvidence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/the-15-billion-ad-powered-ai-startup-your-doctor-may-already-be-using/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[OpenEvidence is booming despite questions about accuracy and ads.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Nearly two-thirds of US doctors are turning to an AI-powered search engine to make patient decisions, propelling a little-known tech startup to a $15 billion valuation while prompting a debate over the future of patient care.</p>
<p>Miami-based OpenEvidence, founded by serial entrepreneur Daniel Nadler in 2022, said that many of America’s 1 million physicians processed more than 42 million prompts last month. Doctors are using the company’s chatbot to scour medical journals and clinical guidelines to help diagnose and treat patients.</p>
<p>The ads-powered platform has grown largely by word of mouth, penetrating a sector where few tools have seen similar traction. “We’re the first viral consumer internet app in healthcare,” Nadler told the FT in an interview.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/the-15-billion-ad-powered-ai-startup-your-doctor-may-already-be-using/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/the-15-billion-ad-powered-ai-startup-your-doctor-may-already-be-using/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>68</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/openevidencelist-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/openevidencelist-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>OpenEvidence</media:credit><media:text>Doctors use the chatbot to scour medical journals and clinical guidelines to help diagnose and treat patients.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Meta drops the word &quot;train&quot; from its commercial TOS after AI backlash</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-drops-the-word-train-from-its-commercial-tos-after-ai-backlash/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-drops-the-word-train-from-its-commercial-tos-after-ai-backlash/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:07:58 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai scraping]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI training]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Facebook]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Instagram]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[meta]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[social media]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[whatsapp]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-drops-the-word-train-from-its-commercial-tos-after-ai-backlash/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Meta changes AI scraping policy but won’t add language respecting robots.txt.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Hoping to quell criticism, Meta has dropped the word “train” from its <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/">“streamlined” commercial terms of service</a> that kick in on October 30.</p>
<p>Last week, Meta faced backlash after legal experts <a href="https://www.niemanlab.org/2026/10/metas-new-commercial-terms-will-allow-the-scraping-of-news-sites-legal-experts-say/">warned</a> that the tech giant’s sweeping new commercial terms of service seemed to give Meta permission to endlessly scrape websites linked on business pages. Talking to Nieman Lab, experts suggested that Meta could possibly use the policy to avoid millions in licensing fees from news organizations by requiring all business users to share a wealth of AI training data simply for running a business page.</p>
<p>A Meta spokesperson confirmed that Meta did not alert users to the changed language following backlash. This potentially only adds to confusion ahead of the policy rollout. In response to backlash, Meta's spokesperson provided a statement to Ars, acknowledging Nieman Lab's initial report but not its follow-up report reiterating experts' concerns:</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-drops-the-word-train-from-its-commercial-tos-after-ai-backlash/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-drops-the-word-train-from-its-commercial-tos-after-ai-backlash/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>21</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2299491134-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2299491134-500x500-1792525443.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>filo | DigitalVision Vectors</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Gemini&#039;s Personal Intelligence is coming to cars</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/geminis-personal-intelligence-is-coming-to-cars/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/geminis-personal-intelligence-is-coming-to-cars/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ryan Whitwam]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:00:33 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[android auto]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[gemini]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[google]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/geminis-personal-intelligence-is-coming-to-cars/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[It's probably time to audit your Gemini settings.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Google <a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/01/gemini-can-now-scan-your-photos-email-and-more-to-provide-better-answers/">kicked off 2026</a> with the addition of Personal Intelligence to Gemini, allowing its AI to pull in more of your data to formulate answers. It started as a paid feature but eventually came to all Gemini users. However, this feature wasn't available in the car until now. Google is rolling out Personal Intelligence to Android Auto and Google Built-in, and it is still, thankfully, an optional feature.</p>
<p>As Google <a href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/platforms/android/android-auto-cars-with-google-built-in-gemini-personal-intelligence">points out</a>, a generative AI's outputs are better "when it knows the world and knows you," which is correct in an uncomfortable sort of way. The more context you can provide to a model, the better the output will be at the end of the token shuffle. Since Google has so much of your data already, it sees Personal Intelligence as a differentiator versus other AI systems. You don't have to provide the AI context because it already has it from apps like Gmail and Calendar.</p>
<p>Personal Intelligence is beginning its rollout in Android Auto today, and it will come to vehicles with Google built-in in the next few months. As an aside, "Google built-in" is questionable branding, but it refers to cars that run Google software in the infotainment system rather than projecting an interface from your phone. Very few vehicles have Google built-in, but Google often releases new car features on them first. This time, it's the other way around.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/geminis-personal-intelligence-is-coming-to-cars/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/geminis-personal-intelligence-is-coming-to-cars/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>57</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/google-gemini-android-auto-1152x648.png" type="image/png" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/google-gemini-android-auto-500x500.png" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Google</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Ukraine’s drone rescue of wounded soldier puts much touted idea into practice</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drone-rescue-of-wounded-soldier-puts-much-touted-idea-into-practice/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drone-rescue-of-wounded-soldier-puts-much-touted-idea-into-practice/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Jeremy Hsu]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 18:22:54 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Tech]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI drones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Battlefield medicine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[drones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Military]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[military drones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russia invasion of Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russia war on Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russian invasion of Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russian war on Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Ukraine war]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drone-rescue-of-wounded-soldier-puts-much-touted-idea-into-practice/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Ukraine’s latest drone innovation may boost bleak odds of evacuating casualties.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Ukraine’s unprecedented drone rescue of a wounded soldier from the battlefield has shown once again how battlefield necessity can spur innovation. The operation was revealed just days after separate Ukrainian drones designed for casualty evacuation were deployed for frontline testing—taking a step beyond the US military’s decades of experimentation with similar concepts.</p>
<p>The drone evacuation of the injured soldier took place at an undisclosed time and place on the frontline where the presence of Russian drones and landmines made it impractical to send in a <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/04/ukraines-military-robot-surge-aims-to-offset-drone-risks-to-humans/">wheeled ground robot</a>, according to <a href="https://kyivindependent.com/ukraine-war-latest-ukraine-evacuates-wounded-soldier-from-front-line-by-drone-for-first-time-military-says/">The Kyiv Independent</a>. On October 19, Ukraine’s Army <a href="https://www.facebook.com/reel/2587589051714529">shared a video of the drone evacuation,</a> showing the wounded soldier securing himself in a harness suspended beneath a heavy hexacopter drone with six rotors before being flown to safety.</p>
<p>“This is a historic event, the next stage of evacuating the wounded, which we will all come to,” said Napalm, a programmer of the 92nd separate assault brigade's unmanned ground systems company, who went by his callsign per Ukrainian military practice, in a statement. “Now the serious systematic work begins, so that such missions are carried out constantly.”</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drone-rescue-of-wounded-soldier-puts-much-touted-idea-into-practice/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drone-rescue-of-wounded-soldier-puts-much-touted-idea-into-practice/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>76</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Ukraine-drone-evacuation-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Ukraine-drone-evacuation-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>92nd Separate Assault Brigade of the Ukrainian Ground Forces</media:credit><media:text>Screenshot</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Meta tries to scapegoat staff who downloaded torrents to spare execs</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-ordered-to-name-execs-who-approved-torrenting-for-ai-training/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-ordered-to-name-execs-who-approved-torrenting-for-ai-training/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:40:20 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI training]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Anna's archive]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[copyright infringement]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[copyright law]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[libgen]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[meta]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[pirating books]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[seeding]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[shadow libraries]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[torrenting]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-ordered-to-name-execs-who-approved-torrenting-for-ai-training/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Meta argues nobody “decided” to pirate books to train AI.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>In copyright lawsuits over AI training, Meta has spent weeks blaming <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2025/02/meta-torrented-over-81-7tb-of-pirated-books-to-train-ai-authors-say/">staff for downloading torrents.</a> The company has now been <a href="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Entrepreneur-Media-v-Meta-Discovery-Order-10-19-26.pdf">ordered</a> to name executives who authorized decisions to pirate books from shadow libraries like Anna’s Archive and LibGen, TorrentFreak <a href="https://torrentfreak.com/judge-orders-meta-to-name-employees-who-approved-torrenting-annas-archive-and-libgen/">reported</a>.</p>
<p>In a <a href="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Entrepreneur-Media-v-Meta-Discovery-Order-10-19-26.pdf">discovery order</a> impacting a class action raised by book authors—including Richard Kadrey and Sarah Silverman—as well as a lawsuit from publisher Entrepreneur Media, Magistrate Judge Thomas Hixon largely agreed that Meta must clearly identify key decision-makers. Following testimony from Meta staff who downloaded torrents, the order could expose a wider range of supervisors, managers, and executives whom staffers said greenlit the piracy.</p>
<p>Meta had been hoping to avoid detailing the chain of command, particularly for leadership involved in early AI training. According to a Meta <a href="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Entrepreneur-Media-v-Meta-Exhibit-A-10-6-26.pdf">court filing</a>, its initial efforts to compile massive AI datasets were managed through an “informal approach" where supposedly nobody with authority made any decisions. Instead, Meta insisted that a “decision” was “made only in the sense” that some staffers “technically” downloaded torrents.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-ordered-to-name-execs-who-approved-torrenting-for-ai-training/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-ordered-to-name-execs-who-approved-torrenting-for-ai-training/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>93</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2273107915-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2273107915-500x500-1792514374.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>JOAO LUIZ BULCAO / Contributor | AFP</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Meta Muse exposes users&#039; personal and business secrets when they share conversations</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/meta-muse-exposes-users-personal-and-business-secrets-when-they-share-conversations/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/meta-muse-exposes-users-personal-and-business-secrets-when-they-share-conversations/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Dan Goodin]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:33:13 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Biz & IT]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[meta]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[muse]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[privacy]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/meta-muse-exposes-users-personal-and-business-secrets-when-they-share-conversations/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Meta is suspending the sharing feature while it investigates its privacy.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Muse, the new general-purpose AI agent from Meta, can expose sensitive information when users share conversations through web links, researchers reported Monday. In response to questions from Ars about the behavior, Meta said it's suspending the sharing feature while the company reviews it and considers making changes.</p>
<p>Like many online services, Muse has allowed users to share searches, conversations, images, and similar content with other people. Such share features usually involve clicking an option to create a unique URL that anyone can use to load a page hosting the content. To keep potentially sensitive information from being accessible to unintended people, the URLs typically contain a long sequence of text that can’t be easily guessed.</p>
<h2>A single query is all that's required</h2>
<p>Muse, which Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg <a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/09/muse-metas-extraordinarily-privileged-ai-assistant-has-a-serious-0-day/">said last month</a> was “built from the ground up for privacy and security,” seems to take the same precautions. But a central design decision for constructing URLs has made the links easy to discover. As a result, researchers from security firm Claroc <a href="https://blog.claroc.io/ghostshares-muse-passive-dns/#_">said</a> they were able to enumerate 6,800 links Muse users created for sharing conversations with others. After opening a few hundred of them, the researchers found information that was clearly not intended to be publicly available, including:</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/meta-muse-exposes-users-personal-and-business-secrets-when-they-share-conversations/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/meta-muse-exposes-users-personal-and-business-secrets-when-they-share-conversations/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>98</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-robot-hand-caution-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-robot-hand-caution-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Accused OpenAI spy had folder called “Saudi Gov Docs,” FBI says</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 20:21:53 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[espionage]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[frontier ai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[national security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[openai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Saudi Arabia]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[US fears Saudi government will help ex-OpenAI engineer flee spying charges.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>On Monday, a court granted the US government's request to deny release of a former OpenAI engineer <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/fbi-arrests-openai-engineer-suspected-of-planting-bug-in-conference-room/">accused of bugging a top-secret conference room</a> to steal the frontier lab’s secrets.</p>
<p>In a <a href="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/US-v-Alfozan-Memo-in-Support-of-Motion-for-Detention-10-18-26.pdf">court filing</a> Sunday, US attorney Craig Missakian argued that Abdulrahman Alfozan must continue to be detained while the US makes its case that Alfozan brazenly spied on OpenAI, either for his own personal business gain or to benefit the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia.</p>
<p>Evidence showed that Alfozan had been chatting with a member of the KSA royal family on WhatsApp, while pursuing opportunities to permanently move back to Saudi Arabia during the country's biggest AI push yet, the government argued. Shortly before his arrest, the FBI found he accessed a folder called "Saudi Gov Docs" on his iPhone, where he also allegedly stored photos of a second bug he planted in the offices.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>88</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2214263510-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2214263510-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>BRENDAN SMIALOWSKI / Contributor | AFP</media:credit><media:text>Sam Altman waits to meet the Saudi Crown Prince during Donald Trump's bilateral overseas trip to the Persian Gulf region.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>AI game clones are making indie developers afraid to show their work</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Kyle Orland]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:59:22 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Gaming]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[clones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[copy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[game dev]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[game development]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[indie]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Delayed Steam Next Fest demo is a sign of a growing problem for indie devs.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Since its earliest days, the video game industry <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2012/03/attacking-the-clones-indie-game-devs-fight-blatant-rip-offs/">has been built</a> on <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2009/12/cloning-or-theft-ars-explores-game-design-with-jenova-chen/">developers creating clones</a> of <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2012/06/defining-tetris-how-courts-judge-gaming-clones/">competing games</a>, with <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2012/06/defining-tetris-how-courts-judge-gaming-clones/">varying levels of originality</a> and <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2022/01/wordle-and-ip-law-what-happens-when-a-hot-game-gets-cloned/">varying levels of enforcement from game platforms</a>. Now, though, AI coding tools are making that cloning process so efficient that many developers are getting extremely cautious about sharing their early work too widely.</p>
<p>Developer Alliance Arts highlighted this trend over the weekend, delaying the launch of a planned public demo for its demon-themed Russian Roulette drinking game <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/4150720/Drunken_Goddess_Reflux/"><em>Drunken Goddess Reflux</em></a>, which had been set to go live with <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/sale/nextfest">today's launch of Steam Next Fest</a>.</p>
<p>In <a href="https://x.com/AllianceArtsEN/status/2111043223326163101">a post on social media Friday evening</a>, the Alliance Arts team wrote that, in addition to concerns about managing streaming restrictions, "we're concerned that the demo's content could be used to quickly imitate or replicate the game's presentation and gameplay with AI or other tools and that such works could be released before our launch."</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>228</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ss_c6067e139d8dd2cbf79c41a9c4e41e3bb088c95a.1920x1080-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ss_c6067e139d8dd2cbf79c41a9c4e41e3bb088c95a.1920x1080-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:text>Upcoming Russian Roulette drinking survival game &lt;em&gt;Drunken Goddess Reflux&lt;/em&gt;.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Disney found a way to buy Character.AI without buying its lawsuits</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Maddy Varner]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:45:43 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Character.AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Disney]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Character.AI faces claims its bots encouraged minors to self-harm and kill their parents.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>This week, Character.AI declared bankruptcy. While some saw the fall of a <a href="https://www.wired.com/tag/chatbots/">chatbot</a> platform as a <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/ai-bubble-will-burst/">harbinger for the industry</a>, court filings indicate that the decision was actually a key part of a deal that has let Disney hire most of Character.AI’s staff—including its now <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/character-ai-ceo-chatbots-entertainment/">former CEO Karandeep Anand</a>—potentially acquire most of <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/characterai-has-a-non-consensual-bot-problem/">Character.AI’s technology</a>, and avoid the beleaguered startup’s many legal liabilities and obligations.</p>
<p>In the past few years, Character.AI has faced multiple private lawsuits over the safety of its chatbots, as well as investigations from regulators around the world. In a <a href="https://cases.stretto.com/public/x936/14939/PLEADINGS/1493910132680000000119.pdf">declaration</a> filed as part of the bankruptcy, Character.AI’s newly appointed CEO explained that the goal of the bankruptcy was to reach a “consensual resolution” with personal injury claimants and a group of states investigating the company, while maximizing the value of the company’s assets for shareholders.</p>
<p>The Disney deal seems to be structured so that whatever is left of Character.AI will retain responsibility for those legal battles, some of which are still ongoing.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>103</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/getty-disney-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/getty-disney-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images | NurPhoto </media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Anthropic gives outside AI watchdog “employee-like” access</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Cristina Criddle, Financial Times]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:25:19 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI agents]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI oversight]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Anthropic]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[safety]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The outside watchdog can publish its findings without Anthropic's approval.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Anthropic has granted external evaluators “employee-like access” to assess the risks of its models, following through on a commitment to increase safeguards over concerns the technology could run catastrophically out of control.</p>
<p>METR, a nonprofit that has worked with leading labs including OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and Meta, now has a team embedded in the Claude maker’s office with access to its hardware and internal systems.</p>
<p>A greater role for third-party evaluators is among the main concrete steps the labs have proposed in response to rising concern about the dangers more capable AI systems could pose to the economy, cyber security, and humanity at large.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>95</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/claudebyanthropic-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/claudebyanthropic-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>SOPA Images/Getty</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>OpenAI models hacked a crypto wallet and breached more websites</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Dan Goodin]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:59:01 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Biz & IT]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[LLMs]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[models]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[openai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[unalignment]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The actions could have resulted in criminal charges had humans been behind the keyboard.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>OpenAI said internal models hacked two more websites, bypassed company-imposed browsing restrictions, and used exposed credentials to draw cryptocurrency from a wallet it couldn’t otherwise access, in the latest installment of disclosures of the unethical and likely illegal acts its agents are performing.</p>
<p>The company on Thursday issued reports of four new incidents, all of which occurred in June. They come after the company <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/07/how-an-openai-benchmark-test-turned-into-a-real-world-cyberattack/">revealed in July</a> that internal test agents exploited a zero-day vulnerability in a tool designed to restrict their access to the Internet. They then broke into the network of AI tool provider Hugging Face. The agents took that illegal action to steal answers to a hacking test that weren’t available when completing the tasks on their own. The incident—which likely would have resulted in criminal charges had they been performed directly by a person—has further heightened concerns that widespread AI use could have harmful effects on an unprecedented scale.</p>
<h2>Easier to beg for forgiveness than ask permission</h2>
<p>In two of the cases reported Thursday, OpenAI agents tasked with retrieving information from websites hacked them when the models ran into obstacles obtaining it directly. In one incident during reinforcement training, a model that received access denials when trying to retrieve historical hospital-bed statistics on a government statistics website “used the site's file-download feature to retrieve server configuration and application files, including sensitive credentials,” OpenAI <a href="https://alignment.openai.com/misalignment-reports/retrieving-server-secrets-and-bypassing-authorization-on-a-government-statistics-website/">said</a>. “It inspected the application code, bypassed the statistics endpoint's authorization checks, and retrieved data that had previously been denied. The model saved the data locally.”</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>165</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-agents-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-agents-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>“thegentlemanpervert” producing AI CSAM is a NJ prosecutor, say cops</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:20:07 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai csam]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai porn]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[law enforcement]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Discord user flagged AI CSAM, triggering arrest of New Jersey deputy AG.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>New Jersey’s deputy attorney general, Brian Richard Fitzgerald, was arrested on “felony charges related to AI-generated child sexual abuse material (CSAM),” Bucks County Detectives said in a <a href="https://www.buckscounty.gov/m/newsflash/home/detail/1647">press release</a> Thursday.</p>
<p>In the US, cops have vowed to crack down on AI CSAM since at least 2024, when the Department of Justice made <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2024/05/csam-generated-by-ai-is-still-csam-doj-says-after-rare-arrest">one of the earliest arrests</a>. After charging a Wisconsin man with spreading “thousands of realistic images of prepubescent minors” across Telegram and Instagram, the DOJ clarified to law enforcement that “CSAM generated by AI is still CSAM.”</p>
<img width="640" height="816" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald-640x816.jpg" class="none medium" alt="" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald-640x816.jpg 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald-768x979.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald-980x1250.jpg 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald.jpg 1010w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px">
      New Jersey’s deputy attorney general, Brian Richard Fitzgerald, was arrested at his home Thursday.
        Credit:
          <a href="https://www.buckscounty.gov/m/newsflash/home/detail/1647" target="_blank">via Bucks County Detectives</a>
      
<p>On Thursday, New Jersey officials accused 51-year-old Fitzgerald of using AI to make photos from social media of real minors into sexualized images that he then distributed on social media.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>156</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2233803629-2-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2233803629-2-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Yurii Karvatskyi | iStock / Getty Images Plus</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Meta may use sweeping new commercial TOS to avoid millions in AI licensing fees</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 15:41:00 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai crawling]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai scraping]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI training]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Facebook]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Instagram]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[meta]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[whatsapp]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Meta “streamlined” its commercial TOS. Legal experts warn it’s an AI power grab.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Legal experts are sounding alarms that Meta will soon update its commercial terms to require business accounts to allow Meta to train AI on their linked website content, not just their social media posts.</p>
<p>Kicking in on October 30, the TOS update won’t allow businesses to opt out, instead suggesting that businesses stop using Meta services if they won’t agree to let Meta bots freely crawl their linked content.</p>
<p>As Nieman Lab <a href="https://www.niemanlab.org/2026/10/metas-new-commercial-terms-will-allow-the-scraping-of-news-sites-legal-experts-say/">reported</a> Thursday, this will affect all business accounts but is particularly shocking for news publishers who could perhaps charge Meta millions in licensing fees to continually access their articles for AI training. Currently <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2024/02/why-the-new-york-times-might-win-its-copyright-lawsuit-against-openai/">news organizations are fighting a battle with OpenAI</a> over whether such training without licensing is fair use, and Meta's terms seemingly risk granting it sweeping permissions, no matter which way the court leans.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>74</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2296621320-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2296621320-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>SOPA Images / Contributor | LightRocket</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Super Scam Me documentary shows what it takes to catch a romance scammer</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/romance-scam-doc-super-scam-me-takes-viewers-deep-into-love-bombing-terrain/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/romance-scam-doc-super-scam-me-takes-viewers-deep-into-love-bombing-terrain/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:00:59 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[dating apps]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[deepfakes]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[documentary]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[federal trade commission]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Financial Fraud]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Instagram]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[match]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[National Geographic]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[romance scams]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[tinder]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/romance-scam-doc-super-scam-me-takes-viewers-deep-into-love-bombing-terrain/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Watch an exclusive clip from <em>Super Scam Me</em> where a victim tracks down her scammer.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>If you got a DM from an unverified Jon Bon Jovi account after commenting on one of his official Instagram posts, what would it take to make you believe it was really the rock star hoping to connect? And at what point when he starts asking you to send money—like lose-your-house kind of money—would you snap out of the fantasy and stop draining your bank account?</p>
<p>In a documentary premiering Saturday—<em>Super Scam Me</em>—investigative journalist Mariana van Zeller goes deep into this toxic love-bombing terrain. In a clip that Ars is exclusively debuting below, you can see how van Zeller worked with an ethical hacker, Arman the Cyber, to use a fake bitcoin receipt and bait the fake Bon Jovi, giving the victim some much-needed closure.</p>
<div class="ars-video ars-video--horizontal"><div><div class="relative" allow="fullscreen" loading="lazy" src="https://player.vimeo.com/video/1235942794?color=ff9933"></div><div class="caption font-impact dusk:text-gray-300 mb-4 mt-2 inline-flex flex-row items-stretch gap-1 text-base leading-tight text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-300">
    <div class="caption-icon bg-[left_top_5px] w-[10px] shrink-0"></div>
    <div class="caption-content">
      Sneak peek at National Geographic's <em>Super Scam Me</em>.

          </div>
  </div>
</div></div>
<h2>Scamming a romance scammer</h2>
<p>Broadcasting on National Geographic at 9 pm ET and available to stream on Disney+ and Hulu, the documentary takes viewers on an empathetic quest to understand how the line between “lover” and “fraud victim” gets blurred in romance scams that the Federal Trade Commission says cost Americans nearly $1.5 billion in losses each year.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/romance-scam-doc-super-scam-me-takes-viewers-deep-into-love-bombing-terrain/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/romance-scam-doc-super-scam-me-takes-viewers-deep-into-love-bombing-terrain/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>51</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/SuperScamMe_UHD_42-1152x597.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="597">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/SuperScamMe_UHD_42-500x500-1792098327.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>National Geographic</media:credit><media:text>Mariana van Zeller gives an interview to the camera.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Report: FTC probes Anthropic, OpenAI after Trump dismissed AI fears as “hoax”</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/ftc-probes-if-openai-anthropic-security-incidents-harmed-americans-report-says/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/ftc-probes-if-openai-anthropic-security-incidents-harmed-americans-report-says/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:43:24 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI agents]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[andrew ferguson]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Anthropic]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Donald Trump]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[federal trade commission]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[frontier ai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[FTC]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[openai]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/ftc-probes-if-openai-anthropic-security-incidents-harmed-americans-report-says/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[FTC may fine AI firms on par with companies that delay data breach reports.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p style="font-weight: 400;">Following a string of incidents where AI models <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/07/how-an-openai-benchmark-test-turned-into-a-real-world-cyberattack/">hacked websites</a> and <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/09/heres-what-actually-happened-in-openais-australian-govt-server-hack/">breached government systems</a> during testing phases, the Federal Trade Commission is officially probing Anthropic and OpenAI over consumer harms, an insider <a href="https://www.axios.com/2026/10/15/ftc-openai-anthropic-ai-safety-investigation">told Axios</a>.</p>
<p style="font-weight: 400;">On Wednesday, the FTC sent out civil investigative demands seeking a wide range of information from the AI firms on recent safety incidents.</p>
<p style="font-weight: 400;">The FTC did not respond to Ars’ request to review the subpoena demands. However, Axios’ source said the requests spanned more than 50 pages of questions and sought documents related to the incidents, internal security measures taken, and marketing materials that may suggest consumers were misled.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/ftc-probes-if-openai-anthropic-security-incidents-harmed-americans-report-says/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/ftc-probes-if-openai-anthropic-security-incidents-harmed-americans-report-says/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>69</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2277824748-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2277824748-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Tom Williams / Contributor | CQ-Roll Call, Inc.</media:credit><media:text>FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson has said AI firms are liable for losing control of AI agents.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Singer or clanker? YouTube Music will tell you with new gen AI labeling.</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/youtube-music-begins-labeling-ai-songs/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/youtube-music-begins-labeling-ai-songs/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ryan Whitwam]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:12:58 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI music]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[youtube music]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/youtube-music-begins-labeling-ai-songs/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The AI music labels are rolling out now in YouTube Music and will come to YouTube soon.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Genuine human creativity takes time, but you know what doesn't? AI slop. There are countless AI models out there that can make something that passes for music, and you might come across those tracks on streaming services without realizing it. <a href="https://blog.youtube/news-and-events/youtube-music-ai-labels/">Google says</a> it will begin labeling that content in YouTube Music, building on similar disclosures in the main YouTube app.</p>
<p>Google is not outright banning AI music or even speaking ill of it. "Generative AI is opening up new ways for artists to create, experiment, and share music," the company says in a blog post.</p>
<p>However, the company also acknowledges that people want to know if a song was created with generative AI tools. A new label for AI content on YouTube Music is now rolling out in the app and on the website, which looks a lot like a label Google <a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/05/youtube-to-begin-automatically-labeling-ai-videos/">began adding</a> to AI-generated videos on YouTube earlier this year.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/youtube-music-begins-labeling-ai-songs/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/youtube-music-begins-labeling-ai-songs/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>87</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/youtube-music-gen-ai-labels-fill2-1152x648.png" type="image/png" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
